    <!-- ARTICLE BODY -->
    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="lede">If you've ever left a noisy restaurant feeling drained — like you'd just run a half marathon for your ears — you've already met the central character of this article. Hearing is the most metabolically intensive task your brain performs, and the way you treat it in your fifties may shape how clearly you think in your seventies.</p>

      <p>For decades, hearing loss was treated as a problem you "got around" — turn the TV up, ask people to repeat themselves, smile and nod. We now know the cost of that compensation isn't acoustic. It's cognitive. Every time the auditory cortex strains to fill in a missing sound, it borrows resources from the parts of your brain that handle memory, focus, and emotional regulation.</p>

      <h2 id="section-1">Hearing is a <em>whole-brain</em> activity.</h2>
      <div class="h-underline"></div>
      <p>Your ear is the antenna; your brain is the radio. The hair cells in your cochlea — about 15,000 of them per ear — translate vibration into electrical signal. Those signals then ride the auditory nerve into a network that touches nearly every region of the cortex: language, attention, memory, even the limbic system that handles emotion. Speech processing happens at extraordinary speed: up to <strong>30 phonemes per second</strong>, roughly 1000× faster than vision.</p>

      <p>When that signal gets noisy or partial, your brain doesn't just hear less — it works harder to hear the same amount. Neuroscientists call this <em>listening effort</em>, and it shows up on fMRI as elevated activity in the prefrontal cortex. The conversation you're having in a coffee shop is, neurologically, also a working-memory task.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h4>The 30-second test</h4>
        <p>If you can hear the words but you can't remember what someone said five minutes after a conversation, that's not a memory problem. That's listening effort eating into encoding. Your brain is too busy decoding to file the meeting away.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="section-2">The hidden cost of "getting by".</h2>
      <div class="h-underline"></div>
      <p>Most adults will wait an average of <strong>seven to ten years</strong> between first noticing hearing changes and doing anything about them. During that window, two things happen, and both are quiet enough to miss:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>The auditory cortex begins to atrophy from underuse — the brain prunes circuits it isn't getting good signal from.</li>
        <li>Neighboring cognitive systems get recruited to compensate, which is fine in the short term and exhausting over years.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The result is a slow, almost invisible trade: you keep functioning, but the cost is paid downstream in the form of <em>cognitive fatigue</em>, social withdrawal, and — over decades — measurable acceleration of age-related cognitive decline.</p>

      <blockquote>
        "The brain is plastic, but it's not free. Every system you let go silent is a system you'll have to fight to get back. Hearing care isn't vanity — it's neurological hygiene."
        <cite>Dr. Lori Halvorson, Founder — DUET<sup class="tm">&trade;</sup> Hearing</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="section-3">What the research actually says.</h2>
      <div class="h-underline"></div>
      <p>A 2020 <em>Lancet</em> commission identified hearing loss as the <strong>single largest modifiable risk factor for dementia</strong> — larger than smoking, depression, or physical inactivity. Subsequent work has been remarkably consistent:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Mild untreated hearing loss is associated with roughly <strong>2× the risk</strong> of cognitive impairment over 12 years.</li>
        <li>Moderate untreated loss raises that to <strong>3×</strong>. Severe untreated loss, to roughly <strong>5×</strong>.</li>
        <li>Adults who treat their hearing loss with appropriate, well-fitted intervention show cognitive decline rates indistinguishable from the general population.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The mechanism is still being debated — some of it is the direct cortical pruning above, some of it is the social withdrawal that compounds it — but the policy implication is settled: hearing intervention is one of the highest-leverage things adults can do for their long-term brain health.</p>

