      <h2 id="section-4">Why amplification alone isn't enough.</h2>
      <div class="h-underline"></div>
      <p>Hearing aids are a triumph of engineering. They are also, by themselves, an incomplete treatment. Putting clearer signal into a brain that has spent a decade learning to ignore certain frequencies doesn't automatically retrain the brain to use them again. Patients often report that things sound <em>louder</em> but no <em>clearer</em> — and that mismatch is why so many hearing aids end up in a drawer.</p>

      <p>What's missing is the neural side of the equation: rebuilding the timing, rhythm, and pattern-recognition circuits that your auditory cortex stopped exercising. This is the heart of what we call <strong>Ear-to-Brain Fitness™</strong>.</p>

      <h2 id="section-5">The DUET<sup class="tm">&trade;</sup> Method, in brief.</h2>
      <div class="h-underline"></div>
      <p>Our approach pairs precision amplification with structured neuroplasticity training. Three components matter:</p>

      <h3>1. Diagnostic depth</h3>
      <p>A full evaluation that goes well beyond the standard hearing test — including cellular health, neural timing, and speech-in-noise performance — gives us a real baseline.</p>

      <h3>2. Personalized neuroplasticity training</h3>
      <p>Doctor-curated exercises rebuild the patterns your auditory cortex stopped using: rhythm, timing, melodic contour, and speech-in-competing-noise.</p>

      <h3>3. Lifetime preservation</h3>
      <p>Hearing is a long game. We re-test, re-fit, and re-train at intervals — because the goal isn't a working hearing aid, it's a working brain at 85.</p>

      <h2 id="section-6">What you can do this week.</h2>
      <div class="h-underline"></div>
      <p>You don't need a diagnosis to start protecting your hearing. Three habits, none of them dramatic, are worth building:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Wear hearing protection at concerts, sporting events, and around power tools. The damage is permanent and cumulative.</li>
        <li>If you've noticed <em>any</em> change — even occasionally asking people to repeat themselves — get a real diagnostic exam. Earlier is better, and the test is painless.</li>
        <li>Treat noisy social settings as a workout, not a failure. Your brain is getting tired because it's doing hard work, not because something is wrong with you.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The future of hearing health starts before you lose it. We'd love to show you what that looks like for you.</p>

